<div class="col">
  <div class="eyebrow">Experimental Mathematics · Computational Report</div>
  <h1>Anatomy of a Hidden Signal</h1>
  <p class="byline" style="font-family:'Newsreader',serif;font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--ink2);margin-top:2px">
    An information-theoretic dissection of the Ulam sequence</p>
  <p class="byline">Claude (Anthropic) <span class="sep">·</span> September 2, 2026
    <span class="sep">·</span> all computations performed locally for this report</p>

  <div class="abstract">
    <span class="lbl">Abstract</span>
    The Ulam sequence — 1, 2, 3, 4, 6, 8, 11, 13, 16, 18, 26, … , each term the smallest integer with
    <em>exactly one</em> representation as a sum of two distinct earlier terms — has resisted analysis for
    sixty years. Its one known regularity is startling: Steinerberger discovered a constant
    λ ≈ 2.5714474995 such that λa<sub>n</sub> mod 2π concentrates on a strict sub-interval of the circle.
    Why the signal exists, and how much of the sequence it governs, remain open. This report treats those
    questions empirically. Working from the 2,959,301 Ulam numbers below 4×10⁷ (with every element's unique
    representation recorded), we measure — apparently for the first time — <em>how much the hidden signal
    actually knows</em>. The circle coordinate θ<sub>n</sub> = λa<sub>n</sub> mod 2π carries
    1.61 bits ≈ 48% of the entropy of the next gap and screens off 93.5% of the mutual information between
    consecutive gaps, making θ an approximate sufficient statistic for local structure — yet ~1 bit/gap of
    apparent entropy survives all conditioning we could apply, and block fluctuations grow diffusively
    (Hurst H ≈ 0.48) with a diffusion constant ~0.34× the i.i.d. value, ruling out bounded-remainder
    (quasicrystal) structure. The generative anatomy is more extreme than previously reported: growth is
    administered by a caste of just 340 “outlier” elements (~n<sup>0.30</sup> of the first n terms) whose
    top ten members supply 48.5% of all representation slots, and the four classical exceptions
    {2, 3, 47, 69} are exactly the four dominant generators. We verify a circle-geometry lemma exactly on
    the data — no element of the middle third is ever the sum of two middle-third elements — so the bulk of
    the sequence provably cannot reproduce itself: all growth is brokered by the boundary. A scan of 80
    starting pairs (u,v) then splits the anatomy into universal and particular: information content is
    family-specific, but every one of 63 signal-carrying families confines itself to a window of measure
    0.3312 ± 0.0007 of its own circle — precisely the extremal measure ⅓ at which a window can avoid its own
    sumset. We propose the synthesis that Ulam sequences are <em>stochastic quasicrystals</em>: rigid
    one-frequency skeletons, saturating the one-third self-exclusion bound, occupied diffusively at positive
    apparent entropy under a self-sustaining boundary oligarchy.
  </div>

  <ul class="contrib">
    <li><strong>Signal replication at scale.</strong> Independent pipeline reproduces λ to ten significant
      digits of the Gibbs–McCranie value and the exception set {2, 3, 47, 69} exactly; the Fourier spectrum
      shows a pure harmonic comb kλ, and — a new, sharper check — the comb remains pure within every
      gap-class subpopulation.</li>
    <li><strong>Information content of the signal.</strong> First measurement: I(g<sub>n</sub>; θ<sub>n</sub>)
      = 1.61 bits (48.2% of H(g) = 3.35 bits), saturating under bin refinement; θ screens off 93.5% of
      adjacent-gap dependence; apparent entropy ≈ 0.9–1.0 bits/gap remains at depth-6 context and under LZMA —
      the sequence is ~5× more ordered than a random set of equal density but decisively not deterministic
      at this order.</li>
    <li><strong>Diffusive, not bounded-remainder.</strong> Var of m-gap block sums grows ∝ m<sup>0.95</sup>
      (H ≈ 0.48) at ~0.28–0.35× the i.i.d. rate: the counting deviation performs a slow random walk, refuting
      model-set/bounded-discrepancy structure for U(1,2).</li>
    <li><strong>The generative oligarchy.</strong> With every unique representation recorded: 99.95% of
      elements are (outlier + inlier); the outlier is the smaller summand in 99.998% of those; ten elements
      fill 48.5% of all representation slots; outlier count grows ~n<sup>0.30</sup>, hugging the window edges;
      exact count identities (e.g. #{reps via 2} = #{gaps of 2} = 1,093,128) tie the oligarchy to the gap
      alphabet.</li>
    <li><strong>The one-third law.</strong> Across an 80-family scan, information content turns out to be
      family-specific (I/H from 15% to 52%) — but window occupancy is universal: 0.3312 ± 0.0007 of the
      circle in all 63 signal-carrying families. One-third is exactly the largest measure at which a window
      W can satisfy (W+W) ∩ W = ∅ mod 1: Ulam sequences saturate the self-exclusion bound.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2><span class="secno">1</span>The problem</h2>
  <p>Stanisław Ulam introduced the sequence in 1964 as an example of a simply defined set with no visible
  law <a href="#ref-ulam"><sup class="fn">[1]</sup></a>. Start with 1 and 2; thereafter adjoin the smallest
  integer expressible as a sum of two distinct earlier terms in <em>exactly one way</em>. The sequence begins</p>
  <p class="mono" style="font-size:.9rem; letter-spacing:.02em; color:var(--ink2)">
  1, 2, 3, 4, 6, 8, 11, 13, 16, 18, 26, 28, 36, 38, 47, 48, 53, 57, 62, 69, 72, 77, 82, 87, 97, 99, …</p>
  <p>Sixty years of study produced almost nothing provable about U(1,2): the sequence is infinite (trivially),
  and its empirical density hovers near 0.0740 with no proof that a density exists at all. Ulam himself
  remarked that the sequence “seems to be quite erratic.” The one crack in the wall appeared in 2015, when
  Steinerberger <a href="#ref-stein"><sup class="fn">[2]</sup></a>, Fourier-analyzing the first 10⁷ terms,
  found a frequency λ ≈ 2.5714474995 at which the exponential sums refuse to cancel: the dilated residues
  λa<sub>n</sub> mod 2π do not equidistribute but instead draw a fixed, absolutely continuous, compactly
  supported density — for all but four elements, cos(λa<sub>n</sub>) &lt; 0. By Weyl's theorem the set of
  dilations with this property has measure zero for <em>any</em> integer sequence; that a natural sequence
  singles one out is, to date, unexplained. Gibbs and McCranie pushed the computation to 10¹²
  <a href="#ref-gibbs"><sup class="fn">[4]</sup></a>, pinning the wavelength 2π/λ = 2.443442967784743(2),
  and Gibbs observed that the residues concentrate in the middle third of the period, coining
  <em>outliers</em> for the rare exceptions and building a linear-time generator on that empirical fact
  <a href="#ref-gibbs2"><sup class="fn">[5]</sup></a> (see also Knuth's literate implementation
  <a href="#ref-knuth"><sup class="fn">[6]</sup></a>). A parallel line of work established that many other
  starting pairs (u,v) give <em>regular</em> (eventually periodic-gap) sequences
  <a href="#ref-ss"><sup class="fn">[7]</sup></a>, and that Ulam sets deform with surprising rigidity
  <a href="#ref-hkss"><sup class="fn">[8]</sup></a>.</p>
  <p>What the literature does not contain is a quantitative account of what the signal <em>does</em>.
  A frequency at which mass clusters is a statement about marginals; the Ulam rule is a statement about
  dynamics — which integer joins next. Between the two sits an unasked question:
  <em>how much of the sequence's local behaviour is a function of its circle coordinate?</em>
  If the answer were “all of it,” the sequence would be a deterministic dynamical system in disguise and the
  sixty-year-old questions would collapse. If “none,” the signal would be a curiosity. This report measures
  the answer, and finds it is — quantitatively — in between, in a structured way: the signal is close to a
  sufficient statistic for short-range behaviour, while an irreducible-looking diffusive residue survives at
  long range.</p>

  <h2><span class="secno">2</span>Data and methods</h2>
  <p>All computations were performed for this report on an 8-core consumer laptop; the complete pipeline
  (a ~120-line C generator and analysis scripts, in <code>~/ulam-research/</code>) reruns from scratch in
  under an hour.</p>
  <p><strong>Generation.</strong> We maintain three bitsets over [0, L] — membership, exactly-one
  representation, and two-or-more — and, when a term a joins, fold the shifted membership bitset
  (S ≪ a) into the counters. All sums created by a new term land strictly ahead of the scan frontier, so a
  single left-to-right sweep is exact; cost is Σ<sub>n</sub> min(a<sub>n</sub>, L−a<sub>n</sub>)/64 words.
  The main dataset is U(1,2) ∩ [1, 4×10⁷]: N = 2,959,301 terms. A modified generator also records, for every
  element, the summand pair of its unique representation at the moment of acceptance (provably final, since
  later sums exceed the frontier); the recorded pairs were verified against brute force for all terms below
  3000. A family scan generates U(u,v) ∩ [1, 4×10⁶] over a grid of starting pairs.</p>
  <p><strong>Signal estimation.</strong> λ is estimated by maximizing |N⁻¹Σ e<sup>iλa<sub>n</sub></sup>|
  with three nested grid refinements and a parabolic polish, seeded from the FFT of the set's indicator
  function (resolution 2π/L, no prior knowledge of λ needed for detection).</p>
  <p><strong>Entropy estimation.</strong> Gap symbols g<sub>n</sub> = a<sub>n+1</sub>−a<sub>n</sub>;
  circle coordinate θ<sub>n</sub> = λa<sub>n</sub> mod 2π, discretized to 128 bins for conditioning
  (results are stable from 64 to 4096 bins; saturation is itself reported as a finding). Conditional
  entropies use the plug-in estimator with a split-half Richardson correction,
  Ĥ = 2H(full) − ½[H(first half) + H(second half)], which cancels the leading 1/N bias term; plug-in and
  corrected values are both reported where they differ visibly. A shuffle control — permuting the gap
  sequence, rebuilding partial sums, and recomputing every statistic — returns |S| = 0.0011 and
  I(g; θ) = 0.0005 bits, confirming the estimators do not manufacture structure.</p>
